D. Proposition 6 identities with exact fractions
========================================================================
minus sheet (3n-1), odd n<=300, L<=60: identity m_L 2^{d_L}/3^L = n prod_{l<L}(1-1/(3m_l)) and R_L(d)=n(1-prod): failures = 0 of 9150 checks
  every factor 1-1/(3 m_l) in [2/3, 33212/33213] = [0.666667, 0.999970] (all in (0,1))
plus sheet (3n+1), odd n<=300, j<=60: identity m_j 2^{d_j}/3^j = n + (1/3) sum_{i<j} 2^{d_i}/3^i: failures = 0
minus sheet, odd n<=300, c_400 = m_400 2^{d_400}/3^400: max = 7.705e+00 (all orbits enter a cycle, c -> 0, R(d) -> n)
rational examples for the factor 1 - 1/(3m):
   m=   1/3 : 1-1/(3m) = 0
   m=   1/5 : 1-1/(3m) = -2/3
   m=  -1/5 : 1-1/(3m) = 8/3
   m=  3/11 : 1-1/(3m) = -2/9
   m=  -3/7 : 1-1/(3m) = 16/9
   m=     1 : 1-1/(3m) = 2/3
   m=    -1 : 1-1/(3m) = 4/3
  T_{-1} orbit of 1/3: (3*(1/3)-1)/2 = 0 -> word ends in 0s, not a halving word (factor 0 excluded)
  T_{-1} odd iterates of 1/5: ['1/5', '-1/5', '-1/5', '-1/5', '-1/5', '-1/5', '-1/5']  (eventually periodic; a negative factor occurs)

